The current interpreter loses source locations during lexing/parsing and evaluates expressions without a call context,
so main can only print the error category/message.

A general solution should:

1. Track source locations
• Add line/column spans to lexer tokens.
• Preserve each expression’s span in the AST.
• Report the failing file, line, and column:
examples/test.lisp:64:14: FormatError: %d expects integer

2. Show the failing source line
  64 | (expect ($ "%d" (div 7 2)) "3")
                       ^^^^^^^
                       
3. Track user-function calls
• Maintain an evaluation call stack.
• Include function names where available, or  <anonymous function>  otherwise.
• Show nested calls when an error propagates:
Traceback:
  examples/test.lisp:64:14
  examples/math.lisp:12:5 in calculate-average
  examples/main.lisp:30:1 in main

4. Preserve error categories
• Keep existing  FormatError ,  TypeError ,  MathError , etc.
• Add location and trace metadata rather than embedding locations into every error message.

5. Cover all failure paths
• Parse errors: file, line, column, and source excerpt.
• Runtime errors: failing expression and call trace.
• IO errors: operation and source location.
• Control-flow errors such as invalid  break / continue .

6. Add regression tests
• Runtime type error reports its source location.
• Nested function failure includes the call trace.
• Parse errors point to the invalid token.
• Existing error messages remain recognizable.
